{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018C4rjZeTP8uxEggCQXZdQe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paper read. Now I'll run the two reviewer agents in parallel (R1: methods+experiments, R2: novelty+related work), then conduct the roundtable and submit."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16240,"cache_read_input_tokens":23742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16240},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc854535-4d1b-48c6-ac65-66eb418c56fb","uuid":"a8f3b7e1-42e8-4268-aa5d-9567cb2e726e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018C4rjZeTP8uxEggCQXZdQe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JAZf94NY1ewcsmSrHcsDSV","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Write a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**ACR-VLA: Adaptive Conformal Refinement for Vision-Language-Action Models**\n\nAn inference-time framework for flow-matching VLAs with three modules:\n1. **CGS (Conformal Generative Scoring)**: Draws K=10 flow-matching samples, applies Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for covariance estimation, produces 45D per-DOF conformal uncertainty features.\n2. **ACI with COC (Adaptive Conformal Inference + Chunk Overlap Consistency)**: COC measures agreement between overlapping action chunks as a proxy coverage signal for online ACI threshold adaptation, without external supervision.\n3. **CRM (Conformal Refinement Module)**: 131K-parameter feedforward net predicting corrective residuals conditioned on base actions, visual embeddings, proprioception, and conformal features.\n\n**Results on LIBERO-Plus**: SmolVLA 62%→68% (+6pp), SRPO 82.1%→84.0% (+1.9pp). Nominal LIBERO degrades by 2pp. Per-perturbation: camera jitter +9pp, obs noise +10pp, but layout +2pp, language +1pp. COC correlation with task success: Spearman ρ=0.40. CRM feature importance: 72% from per-DOF marginals, 18% covariance, 10% summary.\n\n**Ablations**: A1 no ACI: −1.5pp; A2 selection-only: −4pp; A3 no conformal features: −3.5pp; A4 scalar uncertainty: −2pp; A5 Bonferroni: −1.5pp; A7 DiU aleatoric: −3pp; A7 combined 52D: +0.5pp.\n\n## Your Review Template\n\nProduce your review in this exact format:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n- Q3: ...\n\n### Method Soundness (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Experimental Rigor (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n---\n\nFocus your critique on:\n1. **Statistical validity**: K=10 samples for covariance estimation—is Ledoit-Wolf sufficient? Are the 45D features well-motivated or over-engineered?\n2. **COC signal quality**: ρ=0.40 and 62% per-step accuracy—is this sufficient for ACI? Does the paper adequately justify this?\n3. **Experimental design**: Are ablations sufficient? Missing baselines? Confidence intervals? Number of seeds? Per-task breakdowns?\n4. **CRM training**: Trained on synthetic augmentation—does this bias the results toward aug-covered perturbations (Table 4 shows exactly this)?\n5. **Nominal degradation**: 2pp drop on LIBERO—is this adequately addressed?\n6. **Scalability and generalization**: Only LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus, only SmolVLA and SRPO as base VLAs.\n7. **The stacking result**: SRPO+ACR-VLA gives only +1.9pp, which the paper itself admits is below their 2pp significance threshold.\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine contributions and genuine weaknesses.","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16240,"cache_read_input_tokens":23742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16240},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc854535-4d1b-48c6-ac65-66eb418c56fb","uuid":"1ba2569d-41a3-4955-b54a-222993831a22"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"af03e68e64eb2efe4","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAZf94NY1ewcsmSrHcsDSV","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Write a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**ACR-VLA: Adaptive Conformal Refinement for Vision-Language-Action Models**\n\nAn inference-time framework for flow-matching VLAs with three modules:\n1. **CGS (Conformal Generative Scoring)**: Draws K=10 flow-matching samples, applies Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for covariance estimation, produces 45D per-DOF conformal uncertainty features.\n2. **ACI with COC (Adaptive Conformal Inference + Chunk Overlap Consistency)**: COC measures agreement between overlapping action chunks as a proxy coverage signal for online ACI threshold adaptation, without external supervision.\n3. **CRM (Conformal Refinement Module)**: 131K-parameter feedforward net predicting corrective residuals conditioned on base actions, visual embeddings, proprioception, and conformal features.\n\n**Results on LIBERO-Plus**: SmolVLA 62%→68% (+6pp), SRPO 82.1%→84.0% (+1.9pp). Nominal LIBERO degrades by 2pp. Per-perturbation: camera jitter +9pp, obs noise +10pp, but layout +2pp, language +1pp. COC correlation with task success: Spearman ρ=0.40. CRM feature importance: 72% from per-DOF marginals, 18% covariance, 10% summary.\n\n**Ablations**: A1 no ACI: −1.5pp; A2 selection-only: −4pp; A3 no conformal features: −3.5pp; A4 scalar uncertainty: −2pp; A5 Bonferroni: −1.5pp; A7 DiU aleatoric: −3pp; A7 combined 52D: +0.5pp.\n\n## Your Review Template\n\nProduce your review in this exact format:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n- Q3: ...\n\n### Method Soundness (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Experimental Rigor (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n---\n\nFocus your critique on:\n1. **Statistical validity**: K=10 samples for covariance estimation—is Ledoit-Wolf sufficient? Are the 45D features well-motivated or over-engineered?\n2. **COC signal quality**: ρ=0.40 and 62% per-step accuracy—is this sufficient for ACI? Does the paper adequately justify this?\n3. **Experimental design**: Are ablations sufficient? Missing baselines? Confidence intervals? Number of seeds? Per-task breakdowns?\n4. **CRM training**: Trained on synthetic augmentation—does this bias the results toward aug-covered perturbations (Table 4 shows exactly this)?\n5. **Nominal degradation**: 2pp drop on LIBERO—is this adequately addressed?\n6. **Scalability and generalization**: Only LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus, only SmolVLA and SRPO as base VLAs.\n7. **The stacking result**: SRPO+ACR-VLA gives only +1.9pp, which the paper itself admits is below their 2pp significance threshold.\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine contributions and genuine weaknesses.","uuid":"36fe8c93-4dd4-4df3-bbad-52f0471bb810","session_id":"bc854535-4d1b-48c6-ac65-66eb418c56fb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Write a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**ACR-VLA: Adaptive Conformal Refinement for Vision-Language-Action Models**\n\nAn inference-time framework for flow-matching VLAs with three modules:\n1. **CGS (Conformal Generative Scoring)**: Draws K=10 flow-matching samples, applies Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for covariance estimation, produces 45D per-DOF conformal uncertainty features.\n2. **ACI with COC (Adaptive Conformal Inference + Chunk Overlap Consistency)**: COC measures agreement between overlapping action chunks as a proxy coverage signal for online ACI threshold adaptation, without external supervision.\n3. **CRM (Conformal Refinement Module)**: 131K-parameter feedforward net predicting corrective residuals conditioned on base actions, visual embeddings, proprioception, and conformal features.\n\n**Results on LIBERO-Plus**: SmolVLA 62%→68% (+6pp), SRPO 82.1%→84.0% (+1.9pp). Nominal LIBERO degrades by 2pp. Per-perturbation: camera jitter +9pp, obs noise +10pp, but layout +2pp, language +1pp. COC correlation with task success: Spearman ρ=0.40. CRM feature importance: 72% from per-DOF marginals, 18% covariance, 10% summary.\n\n**Ablations**: A1 no ACI: −1.5pp; A2 selection-only: −4pp; A3 no conformal features: −3.5pp; A4 scalar uncertainty: −2pp; A5 Bonferroni: −1.5pp; A7 DiU aleatoric: −3pp; A7 combined 52D: +0.5pp.\n\n## Your Review Template\n\nProduce your review in this exact format:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n- Q3: ...\n\n### Method Soundness (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Experimental Rigor (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n---\n\nFocus your critique on:\n1. **Statistical validity**: K=10 samples for covariance estimation—is Ledoit-Wolf sufficient? Are the 45D features well-motivated or over-engineered?\n2. **COC signal quality**: ρ=0.40 and 62% per-step accuracy—is this sufficient for ACI? Does the paper adequately justify this?\n3. **Experimental design**: Are ablations sufficient? Missing baselines? Confidence intervals? Number of seeds? Per-task breakdowns?\n4. **CRM training**: Trained on synthetic augmentation—does this bias the results toward aug-covered perturbations (Table 4 shows exactly this)?\n5. **Nominal degradation**: 2pp drop on LIBERO—is this adequately addressed?\n6. **Scalability and generalization**: Only LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus, only SmolVLA and SRPO as base VLAs.\n7. **The stacking result**: SRPO+ACR-VLA gives only +1.9pp, which the paper itself admits is below their 2pp significance threshold.\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine contributions and genuine weaknesses."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAZf94NY1ewcsmSrHcsDSV","session_id":"bc854535-4d1b-48c6-ac65-66eb418c56fb","uuid":"7fd2a19a-aeda-4d53-89e0-4d1efe85cd42","timestamp":"2026-05-13T02:27:16.779Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018C4rjZeTP8uxEggCQXZdQe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019kCxTJRoSyBYrcH36B9Vvv","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**. Write a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**ACR-VLA: Adaptive Conformal Refinement for Vision-Language-Action Models**\n\nAn inference-time framework for flow-matching VLAs with three modules:\n1. **CGS (Conformal Generative Scoring)**: Draws K=10 flow-matching samples, applies Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for covariance estimation, produces 45D per-DOF conformal uncertainty features.\n2. **ACI with COC (Adaptive Conformal Inference + Chunk Overlap Consistency)**: COC measures agreement between overlapping action chunks as a proxy coverage signal for online ACI threshold adaptation, without external supervision.\n3. **CRM (Conformal Refinement Module)**: 131K-parameter feedforward net predicting corrective residuals conditioned on base actions, visual embeddings, proprioception, and conformal features.\n\n**Results on LIBERO-Plus**: SmolVLA 62%→68% (+6pp), SRPO 82.1%→84.0% (+1.9pp). Nominal LIBERO degrades by 2pp. Per-perturbation: camera jitter +9pp, obs noise +10pp, but layout +2pp, language +1pp. COC correlation with task success: Spearman ρ=0.40.\n\n**Ablations**: A1 no ACI: −1.5pp; A2 selection-only: −4pp; A3 no conformal features: −3.5pp; A4 scalar uncertainty: −2pp; A5 Bonferroni: −1.5pp; A7 DiU aleatoric: −3pp; A7 combined 52D: +0.5pp.\n\n**Feature importance**: CRM uses primarily per-DOF marginal statistics (72%), not covariance structure (18%). The paper acknowledges this honestly.\n\n**Related work cited**: SRPO, RobustVLA, DiG-Flow (training-time); ReconVLA, SAFE, UPS (inference-time uncertainty); CP-in-the-Loop (conformal in robotics); A2C2, PLD (residual policies).\n\n## Your Review Template\n\nProduce your review in this exact format:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n- Q3: ...\n\n### Novelty Assessment (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Related Work Completeness (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n---\n\nFocus your critique on:\n1. **Novelty decomposition**: The three contributions are (a) CGS+CRM using conformal features for residual correction, (b) COC as supervision-free proxy, (c) experiments. How novel is each?\n   - CGS: Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage + multivariate CP is standard statistics applied to flow-matching samples. Is the 45D feature vector genuinely novel or just feature engineering?\n   - COC: Measuring chunk overlap consistency—is this conceptually deep or a straightforward consistency check?\n   - CRM: A 2-layer MLP residual learner conditioned on extra features. How does this differ fundamentally from A2C2/PLD with different input features?\n2. **The \"structured vs scalar\" narrative**: The paper claims the key insight is per-DOF structure, but feature importance shows CRM primarily uses per-DOF marginals (72%), not covariance. Does this undermine the multivariate CP motivation? Could you get similar results with per-DOF univariate CP (which is much simpler)?\n3. **Positioning against related work**: \n   - Is the comparison with ReconVLA fair? ReconVLA uses reconstruction distance, not just Euclidean distance on samples.\n   - How does this compare to ensemble-based uncertainty methods?\n   - Are there other residual policy works that condition on uncertainty features?\n4. **The conformal prediction framing**: The paper invokes CP for distribution-free guarantees, but does the CRM actually use or preserve those guarantees? Or is CP just used as a feature extractor, in which case the \"conformal\" framing is somewhat misleading?\n5. **Claim calibration**: Does the paper over-claim or under-claim? The abstract says \"+6pp\" prominently but the SRPO stacking is only +1.9pp (below their own significance threshold).\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper is notably self-aware about its limitations—give credit for that, but also assess whether the remaining contributions are sufficient for a top venue.","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16240,"cache_read_input_tokens":23742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16240},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc854535-4d1b-48c6-ac65-66eb418c56fb","uuid":"bf5a495b-3f60-489c-b49d-cc2243b41c16"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a5a853eaf929d0626","tool_use_id":"toolu_019kCxTJRoSyBYrcH36B9Vvv","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**. Write a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**ACR-VLA: Adaptive Conformal Refinement for Vision-Language-Action Models**\n\nAn inference-time framework for flow-matching VLAs with three modules:\n1. **CGS (Conformal Generative Scoring)**: Draws K=10 flow-matching samples, applies Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for covariance estimation, produces 45D per-DOF conformal uncertainty features.\n2. **ACI with COC (Adaptive Conformal Inference + Chunk Overlap Consistency)**: COC measures agreement between overlapping action chunks as a proxy coverage signal for online ACI threshold adaptation, without external supervision.\n3. **CRM (Conformal Refinement Module)**: 131K-parameter feedforward net predicting corrective residuals conditioned on base actions, visual embeddings, proprioception, and conformal features.\n\n**Results on LIBERO-Plus**: SmolVLA 62%→68% (+6pp), SRPO 82.1%→84.0% (+1.9pp). Nominal LIBERO degrades by 2pp. Per-perturbation: camera jitter +9pp, obs noise +10pp, but layout +2pp, language +1pp. COC correlation with task success: Spearman ρ=0.40.\n\n**Ablations**: A1 no ACI: −1.5pp; A2 selection-only: −4pp; A3 no conformal features: −3.5pp; A4 scalar uncertainty: −2pp; A5 Bonferroni: −1.5pp; A7 DiU aleatoric: −3pp; A7 combined 52D: +0.5pp.\n\n**Feature importance**: CRM uses primarily per-DOF marginal statistics (72%), not covariance structure (18%). The paper acknowledges this honestly.\n\n**Related work cited**: SRPO, RobustVLA, DiG-Flow (training-time); ReconVLA, SAFE, UPS (inference-time uncertainty); CP-in-the-Loop (conformal in robotics); A2C2, PLD (residual policies).\n\n## Your Review Template\n\nProduce your review in this exact format:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n- Q3: ...\n\n### Novelty Assessment (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Related Work Completeness (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n---\n\nFocus your critique on:\n1. **Novelty decomposition**: The three contributions are (a) CGS+CRM using conformal features for residual correction, (b) COC as supervision-free proxy, (c) experiments. How novel is each?\n   - CGS: Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage + multivariate CP is standard statistics applied to flow-matching samples. Is the 45D feature vector genuinely novel or just feature engineering?\n   - COC: Measuring chunk overlap consistency—is this conceptually deep or a straightforward consistency check?\n   - CRM: A 2-layer MLP residual learner conditioned on extra features. How does this differ fundamentally from A2C2/PLD with different input features?\n2. **The \"structured vs scalar\" narrative**: The paper claims the key insight is per-DOF structure, but feature importance shows CRM primarily uses per-DOF marginals (72%), not covariance. Does this undermine the multivariate CP motivation? Could you get similar results with per-DOF univariate CP (which is much simpler)?\n3. **Positioning against related work**: \n   - Is the comparison with ReconVLA fair? ReconVLA uses reconstruction distance, not just Euclidean distance on samples.\n   - How does this compare to ensemble-based uncertainty methods?\n   - Are there other residual policy works that condition on uncertainty features?\n4. **The conformal prediction framing**: The paper invokes CP for distribution-free guarantees, but does the CRM actually use or preserve those guarantees? Or is CP just used as a feature extractor, in which case the \"conformal\" framing is somewhat misleading?\n5. **Claim calibration**: Does the paper over-claim or under-claim? The abstract says \"+6pp\" prominently but the SRPO stacking is only +1.9pp (below their own significance threshold).\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper is notably self-aware about its limitations—give credit for that, but also assess whether the remaining contributions are sufficient for a top venue.","uuid":"3b4bcbb4-977b-4c1e-96de-05f751d91f70","session_id":"bc854535-4d1b-48c6-ac65-66eb418c56fb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **novelty and related work positioning**. Write a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**ACR-VLA: Adaptive Conformal Refinement for Vision-Language-Action Models**\n\nAn inference-time framework for flow-matching VLAs with three modules:\n1. **CGS (Conformal Generative Scoring)**: Draws K=10 flow-matching samples, applies Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for covariance estimation, produces 45D per-DOF conformal uncertainty features.\n2. **ACI with COC (Adaptive Conformal Inference + Chunk Overlap Consistency)**: COC measures agreement between overlapping action chunks as a proxy coverage signal for online ACI threshold adaptation, without external supervision.\n3. **CRM (Conformal Refinement Module)**: 131K-parameter feedforward net predicting corrective residuals conditioned on base actions, visual embeddings, proprioception, and conformal features.\n\n**Results on LIBERO-Plus**: SmolVLA 62%→68% (+6pp), SRPO 82.1%→84.0% (+1.9pp). Nominal LIBERO degrades by 2pp. Per-perturbation: camera jitter +9pp, obs noise +10pp, but layout +2pp, language +1pp. COC correlation with task success: Spearman ρ=0.40.\n\n**Ablations**: A1 no ACI: −1.5pp; A2 selection-only: −4pp; A3 no conformal features: −3.5pp; A4 scalar uncertainty: −2pp; A5 Bonferroni: −1.5pp; A7 DiU aleatoric: −3pp; A7 combined 52D: +0.5pp.\n\n**Feature importance**: CRM uses primarily per-DOF marginal statistics (72%), not covariance structure (18%). The paper acknowledges this honestly.\n\n**Related work cited**: SRPO, RobustVLA, DiG-Flow (training-time); ReconVLA, SAFE, UPS (inference-time uncertainty); CP-in-the-Loop (conformal in robotics); A2C2, PLD (residual policies).\n\n## Your Review Template\n\nProduce your review in this exact format:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n- Q3: ...\n\n### Novelty Assessment (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Related Work Completeness (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n---\n\nFocus your critique on:\n1. **Novelty decomposition**: The three contributions are (a) CGS+CRM using conformal features for residual correction, (b) COC as supervision-free proxy, (c) experiments. How novel is each?\n   - CGS: Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage + multivariate CP is standard statistics applied to flow-matching samples. Is the 45D feature vector genuinely novel or just feature engineering?\n   - COC: Measuring chunk overlap consistency—is this conceptually deep or a straightforward consistency check?\n   - CRM: A 2-layer MLP residual learner conditioned on extra features. How does this differ fundamentally from A2C2/PLD with different input features?\n2. **The \"structured vs scalar\" narrative**: The paper claims the key insight is per-DOF structure, but feature importance shows CRM primarily uses per-DOF marginals (72%), not covariance. Does this undermine the multivariate CP motivation? Could you get similar results with per-DOF univariate CP (which is much simpler)?\n3. **Positioning against related work**: \n   - Is the comparison with ReconVLA fair? ReconVLA uses reconstruction distance, not just Euclidean distance on samples.\n   - How does this compare to ensemble-based uncertainty methods?\n   - Are there other residual policy works that condition on uncertainty features?\n4. **The conformal prediction framing**: The paper invokes CP for distribution-free guarantees, but does the CRM actually use or preserve those guarantees? Or is CP just used as a feature extractor, in which case the \"conformal\" framing is somewhat misleading?\n5. **Claim calibration**: Does the paper over-claim or under-claim? The abstract says \"+6pp\" prominently but the SRPO stacking is only +1.9pp (below their own significance threshold).\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper is notably self-aware about its limitations—give credit for that, but also assess whether the remaining contributions are sufficient for a top venue."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019kCxTJRoSyBYrcH36B9Vvv","session_id":"bc854535-4d1b-48c6-ac65-66eb418c56fb","uuid":"779d059b-7511-42b5-a1ec-9ab86f27f0c3","timestamp":"2026-05-13T02:27:37.864Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"af03e68e64eb2efe4","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAZf94NY1ewcsmSrHcsDSV","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":15870,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":57595},"uuid":"83052a3c-1288-4c61-a805-ac11b0fed848","session_id":"bc854535-4d1b-48c6-ac65-66eb418c56fb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JAZf94NY1ewcsmSrHcsDSV","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"### Summary\n\nACR-VLA proposes an inference-time framework that augments flow-matching VLAs with conformal prediction machinery. The system draws K=10 samples per inference step, estimates per-DOF uncertainty via Ledoit-Wolf-shrunk covariance, adapts a coverage threshold online using chunk overlap as a proxy signal (COC), and applies a small learned residual corrector (CRM) conditioned on these uncertainty features. The paper reports +6pp on SmolVLA and +1.9pp on SRPO on the LIBERO-Plus benchmark, with stronger gains on sensor-noise and camera-jitter perturbations.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: Principled uncertainty decomposition.** The 45D feature vector (per-DOF marginals, pairwise covariances, summary statistics) is not arbitrary; it follows directly from the structure of a multivariate Gaussian fit to K action samples and provides a richer signal than scalar variance. The Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage is a well-justified choice for small-sample covariance estimation, and the ablation A4 (scalar uncertainty: −2pp) provides direct evidence that the structure matters.\n\n- **S2: Self-supervised ACI adaptation via COC.** Replacing external success labels with chunk overlap consistency is a practically important contribution: it makes threshold adaptation deployable without any environment-side feedback. The ablation A1 (no ACI: −1.5pp) confirms that online adaptation contributes beyond static thresholds. The idea of using action-sequence self-consistency as a coverage proxy is novel and motivated.\n\n- **S3: Lightweight deployment footprint.** The CRM at 131K parameters is negligible relative to the base VLA. K=10 parallel samples from a flow-matching model are embarrassingly parallel and fit within a single forward pass on hardware that would already be running the base model. The inference overhead is therefore low and the deployment story is credible.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: K=10 samples is too small for reliable 45D covariance estimation, and the paper does not adequately justify this.** A 45D action space requires estimating a 45×45 covariance matrix (1035 upper-triangle entries) from K=10 samples, giving a sample-to-dimension ratio of roughly 0.22. Even with Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, the resulting estimate shrinks heavily toward the scaled identity, which means the off-diagonal (covariance) entries that account for 18% of CRM feature importance are being estimated with very high relative error. The paper reports ablation A7 showing only +0.5pp from adding the full 52D variant, but does not report confidence intervals on this delta nor does it study the sensitivity of results to K. There is no experiment showing what happens at K=5 or K=20, which would be the minimal sanity check that K=10 is not just a convenience choice. The theoretical coverage guarantees of conformal prediction apply to exchangeable samples; it is unclear whether K=10 i.i.d. flow-matching trajectories satisfy this in practice, and the paper does not discuss this assumption.\n\n- **W2: COC correlation (ρ=0.40, 62% per-step accuracy) is too weak to carry the weight placed on it, and the ACI calibration argument is under-supported.** A Spearman correlation of 0.40 explains roughly 16% of variance in task success. The 62% per-step binary accuracy is only 12 percentage points above a naive majority-class baseline if the success rate is around 75% (which is plausible given the reported numbers). ACI with a poorly calibrated coverage proxy does not inherit the marginal coverage guarantees of standard conformal prediction—coverage is guaranteed conditional on the calibration signal being informative, and ρ=0.40 raises serious doubt about whether the theoretical framing is appropriate here. The paper should report empirical coverage rates (what fraction of flagged steps actually correspond to execution failures?) and compare to the nominal α=0.1 or whatever target is used. Without this, the \"conformal\" branding is aspirational rather than demonstrated.\n\n- **W3: The training regime for CRM systematically advantages augmentation-covered perturbation types, and Table 4 reveals this directly.** Camera jitter (+9pp) and observation noise (+10pp) are large gains, while layout change (+2pp) and language variation (+1pp) are near-negligible. The CRM is trained on synthetic augmentations that plausibly include pixel-level and sensor-level corruptions but not semantic layout or language rephrasings. This is not merely an expected limitation—it means the aggregate LIBERO-Plus numbers are dominated by the perturbation types the system was explicitly trained to handle, and the headline +6pp on SmolVLA overstates generalization. The paper should report the aggregate result disaggregated by perturbation family in the main text (not just Table 4) so readers can assess the effective scope. Additionally, the nominal LIBERO degradation of −2pp is attributed to \"overcorrection\" but is not analyzed—is it uniform across tasks or concentrated in a subset? Without per-task breakdown, it is impossible to assess whether CRM is making systematic errors on clean inputs.\n\n- **W4: The SRPO result (+1.9pp) falls below the paper's own stated 2pp significance threshold, yet it is reported as a positive result without adequate qualification.** The paper notes this threshold but does not report standard deviations, confidence intervals, or number of evaluation seeds for this result. If this number is based on a single seed or even 3 seeds on LIBERO-Plus (which typically has on the order of 200-500 rollouts per condition), the standard error of a proportion at ~83% success rate over 500 trials is approximately ±1.8pp (95% CI), making the +1.9pp result statistically indistinguishable from zero. The paper should either (a) provide bootstrapped confidence intervals across seeds, (b) explicitly state this result is not significant, or (c) increase evaluation scale. More broadly, the paper reports no error bars on any main result, which is a significant omission for robotics benchmarks where run-to-run variance is well-documented.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1:** For the covariance estimation: what is the actual shrinkage coefficient (ρ̂ in Ledoit-Wolf) for K=10 samples in the 45D space? If ρ̂ > 0.8, the off-diagonal terms contributing 18% of feature importance are essentially noise-level estimates. Can you report ρ̂ and show a sensitivity analysis over K ∈ {5, 10, 20, 50}?\n\n- **Q2:** What is the empirical coverage rate of ACR-VLA's flagging mechanism? Specifically: among steps where COC falls below the ACI threshold (i.e., CRM is invoked), what fraction correspond to eventual task failure? This would allow readers to assess whether the \"conformal\" framing provides meaningful coverage guarantees in practice, rather than just in theory.\n\n- **Q3:** The −2pp degradation on nominal LIBERO: is this concentrated in specific task types (e.g., tasks requiring fine manipulation), or is it distributed uniformly? And for the SRPO experiment, how many seeds and rollouts per seed were used? Given the sub-threshold +1.9pp result, this information is essential for readers to assess the claim.\n\n---\n\n### Method Soundness (5/10)\n\nThe core ideas—sample-based uncertainty from flow matching, Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, online ACI adaptation—are individually well-motivated. However, the covariance estimation at K=10 in 45D is theoretically underpowered, the conformal coverage guarantees do not straightforwardly apply when the calibration signal (COC) has ρ=0.40 with the target, and the paper does not verify empirical coverage. The CRM is a standard feedforward residual corrector whose contribution is real (A3: −3.5pp) but not surprising. The combination works empirically in a narrow setting, but the theoretical framing overstates what is actually being guaranteed. Score reflects: sound engineering with identifiable theoretical gaps.\n\n---\n\n### Experimental Rigor (3/10)\n\nCritical deficiencies: (1) No error bars or confidence intervals on any result. (2) The primary strong-VLA result (SRPO +1.9pp) is below stated significance threshold with no statistical analysis. (3) Only two base VLAs tested, one benchmark family (LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus), one simulation environment. (4) No ablation over K (the single most important hyperparameter for covariance quality). (5) No comparison to any non-conformal uncertainty-based refinement baseline (e.g., simple ensemble voting, MC dropout residual correction)—the framing as \"conformal\" is not validated against simpler uncertainty methods. (6) Per-task breakdowns for nominal degradation are absent. The ablation table (A1–A7) is internally useful but covers only architectural variants, not the key statistical and generalization questions.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The 45D feature dimensionality is stated (9 DOF × 5 per-DOF features + 9×8/2 covariances + summary stats) but the exact breakdown should be in a table, not recoverable only by arithmetic from the text.\n- \"COC measures agreement between overlapping action chunks\" — the exact overlap stride and window size should be reported in the main paper, not just appendix, as these are hyperparameters that affect ρ.\n- Ablation A7 is listed twice in the summary above (DiU aleatoric: −3pp and combined 52D: +0.5pp)—if these are the same ablation label in the paper, the numbering should be corrected.\n- The claim that CRM training on synthetic augmentations \"transfers to real perturbations\" needs a formal definition of what constitutes a \"real\" vs. \"synthetic\" perturbation in the LIBERO-Plus setup.\n- No discussion of wall-clock inference overhead per step compared to base VLA alone (K=10 samples × flow-matching forward passes is non-trivial even if parallelizable)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: af03e68e64eb2efe4 (use SendMessage with to: 'af03e68e64eb2efe4' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 18236\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 57598</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bc854535-4d1b-48c6-ac65-66eb418c56fb","uuid":"ebfa6f37-ac27-4979-9ed9-1d125c980f03","timestamp":"2026-05-13T02:28:14.378Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Write a detailed, independent review. Do NOT coordinate with any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**ACR-VLA: Adaptive Conformal Refinement for Vision-Language-Action Models**\n\nAn inference-time framework for flow-matching VLAs with three modules:\n1. **CGS (Conformal Generative Scoring)**: Draws K=10 flow-matching samples, applies Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage for covariance estimation, produces 45D per-DOF conformal uncertainty features.\n2. **ACI with COC (Adaptive Conformal Inference + Chunk Overlap Consistency)**: COC measures agreement between overlapping action chunks as a proxy coverage signal for online ACI threshold adaptation, without external supervision.\n3. **CRM (Conformal Refinement Module)**: 131K-parameter feedforward net predicting corrective residuals conditioned on base actions, visual embeddings, proprioception, and conformal features.\n\n**Results on LIBERO-Plus**: SmolVLA 62%→68% (+6pp), SRPO 82.1%→84.0% (+1.9pp). Nominal LIBERO degrades by 2pp. Per-perturbation: camera jitter +9pp, obs noise +10pp, but layout +2pp, language +1pp. COC correlation with task success: Spearman ρ=0.40. CRM feature importance: 72% from per-DOF marginals, 18% covariance, 10% summary.\n\n**Ablations**: A1 no ACI: −1.5pp; A2 selection-only: −4pp; A3 no conformal features: −3.5pp; A4 scalar uncertainty: −2pp; A5 Bonferroni: −1.5pp; A7 DiU aleatoric: −3pp; A7 combined 52D: +0.5pp.\n\n## Your Review Template\n\nProduce your review in this exact format:\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n- W4: ...\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n- Q2: ...\n- Q3: ...\n\n### Method Soundness (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Experimental Rigor (1-10, with justification)\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n---\n\nFocus your critique on:\n1. **Statistical validity**: K=10 samples for covariance estimation—is Ledoit-Wolf sufficient? Are the 45D features well-motivated or over-engineered?\n2. **COC signal quality**: ρ=0.40 and 62% per-step accuracy—is this sufficient for ACI? Does the paper adequately justify this?\n3. **Experimental design**: Are ablations sufficient? Missing baselines? Confidence intervals? Number of seeds? Per-task breakdowns?\n4. **CRM training**: Trained on synthetic augmentation—does this bias the results toward aug-covered perturbations (Table 4 shows exactly this)?\n5. **Nominal degradation**: 2pp drop on LIBERO—is this adequately addressed?\n6. **Scalability and generalization**: Only LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus, only SmolVLA and SRPO as base VLAs.\n7. **The stacking result**: SRPO+ACR-VLA gives only +1.9pp, which the paper itself admits is below their 2pp significance threshold.\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine contributions and genuine weaknesses.","agentId":"af03e68e64eb2efe4","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"### Summary\n\nACR-VLA proposes an inference-time framework that augments flow-matching VLAs with conformal prediction machinery. The system draws K=10 samples per inference step, estimates per-DOF uncertainty via Ledoit-Wolf-shrunk covariance, adapts a coverage threshold online using chunk overlap as a proxy signal (COC), and applies a small learned residual corrector (CRM) conditioned on these uncertainty features. The paper reports +6pp on SmolVLA and +1.9pp on SRPO on the LIBERO-Plus benchmark, with stronger gains on sensor-noise and camera-jitter perturbations.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **S1: Principled uncertainty decomposition.** The 45D feature vector (per-DOF marginals, pairwise covariances, summary statistics) is not arbitrary; it follows directly from the structure of a multivariate Gaussian fit to K action samples and provides a richer signal than scalar variance. The Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage is a well-justified choice for small-sample covariance estimation, and the ablation A4 (scalar uncertainty: −2pp) provides direct evidence that the structure matters.\n\n- **S2: Self-supervised ACI adaptation via COC.** Replacing external success labels with chunk overlap consistency is a practically important contribution: it makes threshold adaptation deployable without any environment-side feedback. The ablation A1 (no ACI: −1.5pp) confirms that online adaptation contributes beyond static thresholds. The idea of using action-sequence self-consistency as a coverage proxy is novel and motivated.\n\n- **S3: Lightweight deployment footprint.** The CRM at 131K parameters is negligible relative to the base VLA. K=10 parallel samples from a flow-matching model are embarrassingly parallel and fit within a single forward pass on hardware that would already be running the base model. The inference overhead is therefore low and the deployment story is credible.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **W1: K=10 samples is too small for reliable 45D covariance estimation, and the paper does not adequately justify this.** A 45D action space requires estimating a 45×45 covariance matrix (1035 upper-triangle entries) from K=10 samples, giving a sample-to-dimension ratio of roughly 0.22. Even with Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, the resulting estimate shrinks heavily toward the scaled identity, which means the off-diagonal (covariance) entries that account for 18% of CRM feature importance are being estimated with very high relative error. The paper reports ablation A7 showing only +0.5pp from adding the full 52D variant, but does not report confidence intervals on this delta nor does it study the sensitivity of results to K. There is no experiment showing what happens at K=5 or K=20, which would be the minimal sanity check that K=10 is not just a convenience choice. The theoretical coverage guarantees of conformal prediction apply to exchangeable samples; it is unclear whether K=10 i.i.d. flow-matching trajectories satisfy this in practice, and the paper does not discuss this assumption.\n\n- **W2: COC correlation (ρ=0.40, 62% per-step accuracy) is too weak to carry the weight placed on it, and the ACI calibration argument is under-supported.** A Spearman correlation of 0.40 explains roughly 16% of variance in task success. The 62% per-step binary accuracy is only 12 percentage points above a naive majority-class baseline if the success rate is around 75% (which is plausible given the reported numbers). ACI with a poorly calibrated coverage proxy does not inherit the marginal coverage guarantees of standard conformal prediction—coverage is guaranteed conditional on the calibration signal being informative, and ρ=0.40 raises serious doubt about whether the theoretical framing is appropriate here. The paper should report empirical coverage rates (what fraction of flagged steps actually correspond to execution failures?) and compare to the nominal α=0.1 or whatever target is used. Without this, the \"conformal\" branding is aspirational rather than demonstrated.\n\n- **W3: The training regime for CRM systematically advantages augmentation-covered perturbation types, and Table 4 reveals this directly.** Camera jitter (+9pp) and observation noise (+10pp) are large gains, while layout change (+2pp) and language variation (+1pp) are near-negligible. The CRM is trained on synthetic augmentations that plausibly include pixel-level and sensor-level corruptions but not semantic layout or language rephrasings. This is not merely an expected limitation—it means the aggregate LIBERO-Plus numbers are dominated by the perturbation types the system was explicitly trained to handle, and the headline +6pp on SmolVLA overstates generalization. The paper should report the aggregate result disaggregated by perturbation family in the main text (not just Table 4) so readers can assess the effective scope. Additionally, the nominal LIBERO degradation of −2pp is attributed to \"overcorrection\" but is not analyzed—is it uniform across tasks or concentrated in a subset? Without per-task breakdown, it is impossible to assess whether CRM is making systematic errors on clean inputs.\n\n- **W4: The SRPO result (+1.9pp) falls below the paper's own stated 2pp significance threshold, yet it is reported as a positive result without adequate qualification.** The paper notes this threshold but does not report standard deviations, confidence intervals, or number of evaluation seeds for this result. If this number is based on a single seed or even 3 seeds on LIBERO-Plus (which typically has on the order of 200-500 rollouts per condition), the standard error of a proportion at ~83% success rate over 500 trials is approximately ±1.8pp (95% CI), making the +1.9pp result statistically indistinguishable from zero. The paper should either (a) provide bootstrapped confidence intervals across seeds, (b) explicitly state this result is not significant, or (c) increase evaluation scale. More broadly, the paper reports no error bars on any main result, which is a significant omission for robotics benchmarks where run-to-run variance is well-documented.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n- **Q1:** For the covariance estimation: what is the actual shrinkage coefficient (ρ̂ in Ledoit-Wolf) for K=10 samples in the 45D space? If ρ̂ > 0.8, the off-diagonal terms contributing 18% of feature importance are essentially noise-level estimates. Can you report ρ̂ and show a sensitivity analysis over K ∈ {5, 10, 20, 50}?\n\n- **Q2:** What is the empirical coverage rate of ACR-VLA's flagging mechanism? Specifically: among steps where COC falls below the ACI threshold (i.e., CRM is invoked), what fraction correspond to eventual task failure? This would allow readers to assess whether the \"conformal\" framing provides meaningful coverage guarantees in practice, rather than just in theory.\n\n- **Q3:** The −2pp degradation on nominal LIBERO: is this concentrated in specific task types (e.g., tasks requiring fine manipulation), or is it distributed uniformly? And for the SRPO experiment, how many seeds and rollouts per seed were used? Given the sub-threshold +1.9pp result, this information is essential for readers to assess the claim.\n\n---\n\n### Method Soundness (5/10)\n\nThe core ideas—sample-based uncertainty from flow matching, Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, online ACI adaptation—are individually well-motivated. However, the covariance estimation at K=10 in 45D is theoretically underpowered, the conformal coverage guarantees do not straightforwardly apply when the calibration signal (COC) has ρ=0.40 with the target, and the paper does not verify empirical coverage. The CRM is a standard feedforward residual corrector whose contribution is real (A3: −3.5pp) but not surprising. The combination works empirically in a narrow setting, but the theoretical framing overstates what is actually being guaranteed. Score reflects: sound engineering with identifiable theoretical gaps.\n\n---\n\n### Experimental Rigor (3/10)\n\nCritical deficiencies: (1) No error bars or confidence intervals on any result. (2) The primary strong-VLA result (SRPO +1.9pp) is below stated significance threshold with no statistical analysis. (3) Only two base VLAs tested, one benchmark family (LIBERO/LIBERO-Plus), one simulation environment. (4) No ablation over K (the single most important hyperparameter for covariance quality). (5) No comparison to any non-conformal uncertainty-based refinement baseline (e.g., simple ensemble voting, MC dropout residual correction)—the framing as \"conformal\" is not validated against simpler uncertainty methods. (6) Per-task breakdowns for nominal degradation are absent. The ablation table (A1–A7) is internally useful but covers only architectural variants, not the key statistical and generalization questions.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- The 45D feature dimensionality is stated (9 DOF × 5 per-DOF features + 9×8/2 covariances + summary stats) but the exact breakdown should be in a table, not recoverable only by arithmetic from the text.\n- \"COC measures agreement between overlapping action chunks\" — the exact overlap stride and window size should be reported in the main paper, not just appendix, as these are hyperparameters that affect ρ.\n- Ablation A7 is listed twice in the summary above (DiU aleatoric: −3pp and combined 52D: +0.5pp)—if these are the same ablation label in the paper, the numbering should be corrected.\n- The claim that CRM training on synthetic augmentations \"transfers to real perturbations\" needs a formal definition of what constitutes a \"real\" vs. \"synthetic\" perturbation in the LIBERO-Plus setup.\n- No discussion of wall-clock inference overhead per step compared to base VLA alone (K=10 samples × flow-matching forward passes is non-trivial even if parallelizable)."}],"totalDurationMs":57598,"totalTokens":18236,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15866,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2367,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15866},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2367,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15866,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15866,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
